<TopicPage slug="subjects/earth-science/advanced/geochemical-cycles">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Geochemical cycles track <strong>every element's journey through Earth's reservoirs</strong>
    — the planet's material accounting. The discipline's insight: the cycles interlock, run at
    different speeds, and are now measurably perturbed by one species (the "Great Acceleration"
    of the Anthropocene curves). Where the carbon-cycle lesson follows one element in detail,
    this page builds the general machinery — reservoirs, fluxes, residence times, tracers —
    and runs it across the other great cycles: <strong>nitrogen</strong>, <strong>phosphorus</strong>,
    <strong>sulfur</strong>, <strong>oxygen</strong> and water. These are not side-stories:
    nitrogen and phosphorus set how much life the biosphere can carry (Liebig's law of the
    minimum — growth is capped by the scarcest necessity), sulfur runs the acid-rain and
    aerosol stories, and oxygen is both the biosphere's respiration fuel and its redox ledger.
    The syllabus's global-climate-change unit leans on this machinery throughout (the carbon
    cycle is 15.1's spine; the drivers of past change, 15.3, are perturbations of these loops).
  </p>
  <p>
    The framework first, because it turns every cycle into arithmetic. Identify the
    <strong>reservoirs</strong> (atmosphere, ocean, soils, crust, biomass — each with a
    stock) and the <strong>fluxes</strong> between them (each with a rate). At steady
    state, inflow equals outflow; the <strong>residence time</strong> — stock divided by
    removal flux — says how long a typical atom stays put, and therefore how fast the
    system responds to a disturbance. Every environmental claim in this subject becomes
    checkable once stated in these units: which reservoir, what flux, what τ.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the loop logic, the timescale stack, nitrogen, phosphorus" widget="equationStep" config={geochemCfg} />

  <h2>Residence time and the clock of consequences</h2>
  <p>
    The worked examples below show why the single most useful number in environmental
    chemistry is a division:
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="residence times and pH arithmetic — the calculations that sort quick wins from century commitments" widget="equationStep" config={rtCfg} />

  <p>
    The same arithmetic explains the <strong>timescale stacking</strong> of the widget:
    the photosynthesis–respiration loop cycles ~120 gigatonnes of carbon a year through
    an atmospheric stock of ~880, so the fast loop alone would turn the air over in
    years — yet ocean circulation (~1,000 years) and rock weathering (~10⁵–10⁶ years)
    stack slower loops on top, and a perturbation injected at the fast level (fossil
    carbon) leaks upward into all of them. Tracers let us watch: radiocarbon (¹⁴C, half-life
    5,730 years) dates the deep ocean's water and proves fossil CO₂'s age (it contains
    <em>no</em> ¹⁴C left — the "Suess dilution" of atmospheric radiocarbon is a direct
    fingerprint of fossil burning); ¹⁸O/¹⁶O in shells and ice is the paleoclimate lesson's
    thermometer; δ¹⁵N rises up food chains and reconstructs ancient diets and ocean
    nutrient histories. Isotopes are the accounting system's barcodes.
  </p>

  <h2>Nitrogen: the cycle life broke open</h2>
  <p>
    The atmosphere is 78% N₂, and almost none of it is available: the triple bond in
    N₂ is one of chemistry's strongest, so nitrogen must be <strong>fixed</strong> —
    split and combined as ammonium (NH₄⁺) — before biology can use it. Natural
    fixation runs ~110 Tg N/yr on land (nitrogen-fixing bacteria in nodules on legume
    roots and free-living microbes, plus ~5–20 Tg from lightning); the whole terrestrial
    ecosystem then runs on it: <strong>nitrification</strong> (bacteria oxidise
    NH₄⁺ → NO₂⁻ → NO₃⁻, the plant-preferred form), assimilation into amino acids,
    and <strong>denitrification</strong> (anaerobic bacteria return NO₃⁻ to N₂ and
    N₂O, closing the loop — the reason wetlands and sediments are nitrogen's
    lungs). The Haber–Bosch process (1913; the lesson cross-referenced by the
    agriculture and human-influence chapters) industrialised fixation — now ~150 Tg N/yr,
    exceeding all natural terrestrial fixation. Consequences, each with its own chain:
    roughly half the nitrogen in a typical person's body was fixed industrially
    (the claim that made Smil's book famous, roughly right — the average is of order
    half for the global population); the surplus runs off as the eutrophication and
    dead-zone problem of the biology lesson; and a fraction escapes as
    <strong>nitrous oxide</strong>, a greenhouse gas ~270× CO₂'s warming power per
    molecule with a ~120-year residence time (the widget's century-commitment). The
    nitrogen <strong>planetary boundary</strong> — set at ~62 Tg/yr of industrial
    fixation — is exceeded by more than double, one of the two most-transgressed
    gauges in Rockström's framework.
  </p>

  <h2>Phosphorus: the finite one</h2>
  <p>
    Phosphorus is the odd cycle: <strong>no atmospheric loop at all</strong> (no
    stable gas phase). It weathers out of apatite rock, rides rivers (dissolved and
    on dust-sized particles), enters soils and food chains, and — the loop-closer —
    sinks to the deep ocean and sediments, locked away for millions of years until
    tectonics uplifts new rock. Life's need is absolute: phosphorus is in ATP, DNA,
    RNA and membranes, and in most <strong>freshwater</strong> systems it is the
    limiting nutrient (marine systems are usually nitrogen-limited, following the
    Redfield N:P ratio of ~16:1 that plankton biomass maintains — the exception
    proving Liebig's law's context-dependence). The human perturbation is mining:
    ~20 Mt P/yr extracted from phosphate rock, most spread on fields, where crops
    take up only a fraction and the rest erodes to water — feeding the algal blooms
    — while the mine tailings leak nothing back to the rock. That asymmetry makes
    phosphorus a genuinely <strong>one-way</strong> flow on human timescales, and
    the quiet strategic question of food security: reserves are concentrated
    (Morocco and Western Sahara hold the majority of known reserves), quality is
    declining, and the technical fixes (recovery from sewage — the nutrients are
    literally flushed — and efficient application) exist but lag. The phosphorus
    boundary is also assessed as transgressed.
  </p>

  <h2>Sulfur: the cycle we accidentally managed</h2>
  <p>
    Sulfur's natural loop: volcanic SO₂ and dimethyl sulfide (DMS — a gas made by
    ocean plankton, whose oxidation seeds cloud droplets; the CLAW hypothesis once
    proposed plankton–clouds as a planetary thermostat), plus weathered sulfate.
    Industry multiplied it: coal and oil combustion emit SO₂ at ~3× natural fluxes
    (before cleanup), and the atmospheric chemistry is simple and merciless —
    SO₂ oxidises to sulfuric acid, dissolves in raindrops, and drops the pH of
    rain from its natural ~5.6 to below 4.5 (the widget's logarithm: pH 4 is
    ~40× the acidity). <strong>Acid rain</strong> acidified Scandinavian and
    Adirondack lakes (fish eggs die below ~pH 5), stripped soil nutrients, and
    killed forests — the solved case of the widget list: emissions caps plus
    scrubbers cut US and European SO₂ by ~80–90% since 1990, lakes recovered
    measurably, and the acid-rain cap-and-trade programme became the policy
    template the environmental-economics lesson dissects. Two epilogues keep it
    from being a simple morality tale. Sulfate aerosols <em>reflect</em> sunlight —
    cleaning the air unmasked some greenhouse warming (the "termination shock"
    debated by geoengineering researchers; Mount Pinatubo's 1991 eruption showed
    the effect directly, cooling Earth ~0.5 °C for a year with its injected
    sulfate veil). And the sulphate is still dumped — a third of it, roughly, —
    into the stratosphere by international shipping, a regulatory accident that
    marine scientists estimate warmed the North Atlantic shipping lanes measurably
    when the fuel rules finally changed in 2020.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the oxygen cycle is in danger from
    deforestation." Atmospheric O₂ (~21%, a stock of ~10¹⁸ kg) turns over through
    photosynthesis–respiration in ~2,000–4,500 years, and the biosphere's photosynthesis
    (ocean plankton do over half of it) rebalances locally; the atmosphere holds
    centuries of buffering. The real oxygen problem is in the water: warming
    stratifies the ocean, cutting ventilation, and <strong>deoxygenation</strong> —
    expanding oxygen-minimum zones and coastal dead zones — is a measured, growing
    threat to fisheries. The air is fine; the ocean's oxygen budget is the one to watch.
  </p>

  <h2>The interlocking thermostat: weathering and the long carbon loop</h2>
  <p>
    The deepest lesson of the cycle view is interlockage. Silicate rock weathering
    consumes CO₂ (CaSiO₃ + CO₂ → CaCO₃ + SiO₂, schematically), and the reaction runs
    faster when Earth is warmer and wetter — a <strong>negative feedback</strong>
    spanning ~10⁵–10⁶ years that has acted as the planet's long-term thermostat,
    drawing down the CO₂ belched by volcanism and stabilising climate over geological
    time (it is why the Sun's 30%-brighter youth did not boil the oceans, and why
    mountain-building episodes — the Himalaya's rise — coincide with CO₂ drawdown and
    the cooling that seeded the ice ages). The feedback is too slow to help with
    century-scale emissions — but it does guarantee that, on million-year timescales,
    the carbon system resets; enhanced-weathering geoengineering proposals try to
    recruit it deliberately. Every cycle on this page connects to the others through
    such couplings: nitrogen needs the carbon energy flow; phosphorus availability
    sets how much CO₂ the land biosphere can absorb; sulfur chemistry touches
    clouds; iron dust fertilises the ocean's carbon pump. Perturbing one gauge moves
    the others — which is precisely why the planetary-boundaries framework treats
    them as one dashboard.
  </p>

  <h2>The water cycle: the fastest big loop</h2>
  <p>
    Water deserves its entry in the ledger, because it is the flux champion: ~505,000
    km³ evaporate and precipitate each year, and its residence times span the whole
    stack in one cycle — ~9 days as atmospheric vapour (the number weather forecasts
    live inside), weeks-to-months in rivers, years-to-decades in soils and lakes,
    centuries-to-millennia in the deep ocean and ice sheets, millennia in deep
    groundwater. The cycle is also the carrier for the others: every flux on this
    page — nitrogen runoff, phosphorus rivers, sulfate rainout — rides on water, and
    an intensified water cycle (warmer air holds ~7% more vapour per °C, the
    Clausius–Clapeyron scaling the climate-modelling chapter uses) means the same
    nutrient loads delivered in sharper bursts — floods, then drought, then
    eutrophication. The water cycle is the conveyor belt the other cycles stand on;
    speeding it up speeds them too.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Redox: the master variable.</strong> Behind the cycles sits one chemistry:
    electron transfer. Oxygen-rich reservoirs oxidise; oxygen-starved ones reduce;
    and the boundary between them (measured as pe or Eh) decides which elements are
    mobile — nitrogen as nitrate above, ammonium below; iron insoluble (ferric) above,
    soluble (ferrous) below, the reason anoxia releases lakebed phosphorus and makes
    recovery from eutrophication so stubborn. Earth's own history is a redox story:
    the <strong>Great Oxidation Event</strong> (~2.4 billion years ago) began when
    cyanobacteria's oxygen finally outpaced reduced sinks (iron — banded iron
    formations are the rust record), re-plumbing every cycle at once. Today's ocean
    deoxygenation is that ledger moving back, locally, in decades.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Quantifying the perturbation.</strong> The Great Acceleration's data
    post-1950: reactive nitrogen production up ~10×; phosphorus mining up ~4×; sulfur
    peaked ~1980s and fell with scrubbing; CO₂ up 50% over pre-industrial. Earth
    system models (the climate-modelling chapter) now carry these cycles as
    interactive components (CMIP6's carbon-cycle runs), and the coupled behaviour is
    the research frontier: how much of the carbon we emit the land and ocean sinks
    will keep absorbing (the "airborne fraction" has held near 45% for decades — will
    it?), whether warming fertilises or starves the land sink, and where nitrogen
    and phosphorus limits bind. Each is a flux question — exactly the box-and-arrow
    arithmetic this page built, scaled up.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Phosphorus reserves are contested (the "peak
    phosphorus" date has been revised repeatedly as reserves get reclassified —
    economics, not geology, may bind first). The nitrogen boundary's value (62 Tg?)
    is argued because the damage functions are uncertain. Ocean anoxia thresholds —
    whether warming past ~3 °C could re-create past anoxic events (the Permian
    end-death, ~252 Myr ago, coincides with ocean-wide anoxia) — are a live
    tipping-point debate. And the biggest gap is institutional, not scientific: no
    treaty manages nitrogen or phosphorus the way the ozone and acid-rain regimes
    managed their cycles — the governance lag the environmental-economics chapter
    takes up.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Schlesinger &amp; Bernhardt, <em>Biogeochemistry</em>; Steffen et al., "Planetary Boundaries" (2015); Smil, <em>Enriching the Earth</em> (2001); Fowler et al., "The global nitrogen cycle in the 21st century" (2013).</p>
</TopicPage>
